<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Passed to codesign by every path that signs this binary: `make install` (ad-hoc,
    falling back to no entitlements if that fails), `make install-signed` and
    `make release-signed` (an older `sign-developer-id` target was removed in #123).
    The entitlement takes effect only under the hardened runtime, which the ad-hoc
    `make install` does not enable, so there it changes nothing.

    apple-events is required, not optional, once the binary is signed with the
    hardened runtime: every command in this tool reaches Safari through an Apple
    Event, and the hardened runtime blocks those by default. che-ical-mcp #154
    is the cautionary case — tccd refuses to even *show* a prompt for a
    hardened-runtime binary missing the matching entitlement, so the denial is
    silent and permanent while every status API still reports green.

    Accessibility and Screen Recording have no entitlement of their own; they
    are granted per-binary in System Settings and requested by
    `safari-browser setup`.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
